; A SWAP IS A SWAP, NOT A COPY.
; inputs: 0 1 2
; expect: 73 74 75
;
; Two reads before two stores. The program is correct as written; the compiler
; was substituting each read INTO a store position, so Go emitted
;
;   b[0] = b[1]
;   b[1] = b[0]        <- reads what it just overwrote
;
; and a swap silently became a copy. ADR 0010 denies exchange precisely so that
; cannot happen, and ADR 0018 says a buffer read is impure -- but nothing in a
; term says which bound variables are buffers, since an ARRAY read has the same
; shape and is genuinely pure. The rule therefore tests the DESTINATION: a read
; does not move into a body that has effects to be reordered against.
;
; EVERY TARGET AGREED AND ALL OF THEM WERE WRONG, so this case could never have
; FOUND the bug -- it is here to keep it fixed. What found it was writing a sort
; for a map's `keys`, which is the argument for writing programs and not only
; construct tests.
;
; [3,7] swapped is [7,3], printed as 73 plus the input so the three rows differ.
(def run (fn (n)
  (let (build 2 (fn (b)
         (let (set (set b 0 3) 1 7) (fn (b)
           (let (b 0) (fn (vx)
             (let (b 1) (fn (vy)
               (set (set b 0 vy) 1 vx)))))))))
    (fn (t) (@add (@mul 10 (t 0)) (@add (t 1) n))))))
